  "metadata": {
    "title": "SpaceX president Gwynne Shotwell floats Tesla merger on IPO day",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "SpaceX President Gwynne Shotwell declined to rule out a future merger with Tesla on Friday, the day SpaceX began trading on the Nasdaq, saying the two Elon Musk-led companies share overlapping goals. Speaking to CNBC, Shotwell said the two companies are heading in the same direction. \"There's no question that there are synergies between Tesla and SpaceX in our futures,\" she said. \"There's a convergence of what we're all trying to accomplish in the future.\"\nShotwell quipped that combining the two companies \"might make Elon's life a little easier,\" while tempering expectations about timing. She went on to outline her current priorities: \"Right now I'm focused on keeping the lights on here, keeping rockets in production, flying rockets, flying people, getting to the International Space Station, and critically providing broadband to folks that don't have access.\"\nThe comments came as SpaceX completed its IPO, raising $75 billion at a valuation of $1.77 trillion — the largest public offering on record. The company trades under the ticker SPCX. Merger speculation intensified after an amended IPO filing revealed that SpaceX may issue \"significant equity\" to finance future deals. Shotwell separately said that mergers and acquisitions are \"in the future, especially when you look at the AI world.\"\nThe two companies already have deep commercial ties. Tesla holds a stake in SpaceX, the two are jointly developing a chip manufacturing project called Terafab, and SpaceX spent $131 million on Cybertrucks in 2025, according to SpaceX's IPO filing. As Quartz has previously reported, Tesla has sold roughly $890 million worth of vehicles and batteries to SpaceX and its subsidiary xAI since 2023. SpaceX absorbed Musk's xAI — including the Grok chatbot and the X social media platform — earlier this year.\nWall Street analysts are split on whether a merger would follow. At Wedbush Securities, analyst Dan Ives has argued that the odds of a Tesla-SpaceX deal closing in 2027 stand between 80% and 90%, calling the potential combination the \"holy grail\" of Musk's push into AI. Oppenheimer took the opposite view, saying independence between the two companies aligns better with what Musk is ultimately trying to build, even as the firm left the door open, calling a future transaction \"plausible,\" according to Yahoo Finance.\nThe IPO places Musk in the position of running two publicly traded companies simultaneously. Under the terms set out in SpaceX's filing, Musk commands a supermajority of voting shares — more than 80% — and retains the right to sign off even on any board action that could result in his ouster.",
    "text_blocks": [
      "SpaceX President Gwynne Shotwell declined to rule out a future merger with Tesla on Friday, the day SpaceX began trading on the Nasdaq, saying the two Elon Musk-led companies share overlapping goals. Speaking to CNBC, Shotwell said the two companies are heading in the same direction. \"There's no question that there are synergies between Tesla and SpaceX in our futures,\" she said. \"There's a convergence of what we're all trying to accomplish in the future.\"",
      "Shotwell quipped that combining the two companies \"might make Elon's life a little easier,\" while tempering expectations about timing. She went on to outline her current priorities: \"Right now I'm focused on keeping the lights on here, keeping rockets in production, flying rockets, flying people, getting to the International Space Station, and critically providing broadband to folks that don't have access.\"",
      "The comments came as SpaceX completed its IPO, raising $75 billion at a valuation of $1.77 trillion — the largest public offering on record. The company trades under the ticker SPCX. Merger speculation intensified after an amended IPO filing revealed that SpaceX may issue \"significant equity\" to finance future deals. Shotwell separately said that mergers and acquisitions are \"in the future, especially when you look at the AI world.\"",
      "The two companies already have deep commercial ties. Tesla holds a stake in SpaceX, the two are jointly developing a chip manufacturing project called Terafab, and SpaceX spent $131 million on Cybertrucks in 2025, according to SpaceX's IPO filing. As Quartz has previously reported, Tesla has sold roughly $890 million worth of vehicles and batteries to SpaceX and its subsidiary xAI since 2023. SpaceX absorbed Musk's xAI — including the Grok chatbot and the X social media platform — earlier this year.",
      "Wall Street analysts are split on whether a merger would follow. At Wedbush Securities, analyst Dan Ives has argued that the odds of a Tesla-SpaceX deal closing in 2027 stand between 80% and 90%, calling the potential combination the \"holy grail\" of Musk's push into AI. Oppenheimer took the opposite view, saying independence between the two companies aligns better with what Musk is ultimately trying to build, even as the firm left the door open, calling a future transaction \"plausible,\" according to Yahoo Finance.",
      "The IPO places Musk in the position of running two publicly traded companies simultaneously. Under the terms set out in SpaceX's filing, Musk commands a supermajority of voting shares — more than 80% — and retains the right to sign off even on any board action that could result in his ouster."
    ],
    "html": null
  },
